00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in 2017 hulu released the handmaid's  tale a show about a near-future society  in which a totalitarian group has  overthrown the us government adapted  from the 1985 margaret atwood novel the  series became an instant sensation but  even die hard fans may not know the  whole story  series creator bruce miller is no  stranger to writing for television he's  worked on a slew of popular tv shows  such as er everwood and the 100 but the  handmaid's tale was the first show he  created miller had been imagining how to  adapt the story on screen long before he  was able to make it a reality in an  interview with the dream cage miller  explained that he was assigned to the  book in college for a class he said i  loved the story i was always a big fan  of books that built the post-apocalyptic  world after school i read it a couple of  times over the years margaret atwood was  really one of the people that taught me  how to write and how to think like a  writer as my career moved into tv i  thought oh this would make a great tv  show explaining that he was fascinated  by the thought of diving into all the  details in the book miller saw a larger  world ripe for exploration but more than  anything he was attracted to the  characters atwood had created he  explained it all goes back to the people  who live in this world and how they  survive and how they're trapped by their  own choices i wanted to tell the story  about how these people lived through the  experience and how they came out the  other side alive and with some of their  identities still intact that was the  thing i loved about the book realizing  by the end that offred has kept herself  alive  along with being adapted for the stage a  handful of times including at the  haymarket theater in the uk in 2002 the  handmaid's tale also has a film  adaptation the 1990 film was directed by  volker schlondorff and starred natasha  richardson as alfred whose real name is  kate in this version faye dunaway  co-starred as serena joy with robert  duvall as the commander and victoria  tennant as aunt lydia the film loosely  sticks to the events of the book though  the last act plays out a bit differently  much as it does in the first season of  the series but unlike the series the  film wasn't well received it has a 32  rating on rotten tomatoes while the 2017  series scored a 94 for its first season  in a retrospective look back at the film  around the time the hulu series debuted  the movie buff summed up the movie's  review saying schlondorf's treatment is  idly drab and antiseptic indifferent  almost as though he felt atwood's vision  was cinematic enough  the design team for the handmaid's tale  tv series doesn't let any space go to  waste making sure each room of the set  is ripe with deeper meaning and messages  some examples include the commander's  office which offred frequently visits in  season one it's filled with things that  are off limits to women like books art  and alcohol in a similar vein alfred's  bedroom is a reminder of a life that she  can't have  if we're caught reading  they'll cut off a finger  second offense just  the land  in a 2017 interview with architectural  digest production designer julie bergoff  explained some of the meanings behind  their set designs saying we put a desk  there but she can't write so it's almost  like a remnant a remembrance of oh i was  a writer an editor i can't even sit and  write anymore they don't want you to be  vain anymore so we basically put the  shape of a mirror on the wall to make it  feel like at one point there was a  mirror there  while margaret atwood's 1985 handmaid's  tale novel is a work of fiction many of  the horrific details of the book are  pulled from real-life examples of  authoritarian treatment toward women in  a 2017 interview with the guardian  atwood said when it first came out it  was viewed as being far-fetched however  when i wrote it i was making sure i  wasn't putting anything into it that  human beings had not already done  somewhere at some time stylist published  an article with real-life historical  terrors that atwood pulled from like the  enforced dress code the handmaids of  gilead are forced to wear stylist wrote  religious clothing can be and still is  forced upon women by authorities and  other more conservative communities the  amish for example insists that women  wear long dresses made of dark fabrics  and head coverings every single day  another example is the enforced  surrogacy for the sake of the most  privileged getting to raise children  stylists reported in australia as  recently as the 70s indigenous children  were lawfully stolen from their homes  and placed in religious institutions or  fostered out to white families from the  end of world war ii up until the late  70s similar programs were carried out in  the usa and canada when asked about  real-life forced pregnancy in an  interview on the view atwood said which  century would you like to visit which  country would you like to visit  which state in the united states would  you now like to visit  in the novel the handmaid's tale all  people of color have been forced out of  gilead leaving only white central  characters and there aren't any lgbtq  characters in that woods novel either  but this isn't the case in the  television series a change show creator  bruce miller found vital he told time in  2017 what's the difference between  making a tv show about racists and  making a racist tv show where you don't  hire any actors of color miller also  told the hollywood reporter  real diversity actually is  the way to actually use those experience  because i think when you have a singular  voice in the room it there's no one to  challenge right  but the ways in which the show deals  with race is one of the biggest  criticisms it faces in 2017 the guardian  pointed out that what the handmaids go  through is eerily similar to what black  women went through on a daily basis  during slavery yet that parallel is  ignored in the show the guardian  reported none of these details are the  inventions of atwood's imagination or  embellishments from miller's writer's  room this is what actually happened  during 245 years of slavery in the u.s  albeit to black women rather than white  ones vulture also addressed this issue  in 2017 writing the show doesn't end up  considering the racial dynamics of  june's family or what it means to be a  handmaid of color in reality though it's  more concerned with the interiority of  white women at the expense of people of  color who recognize that gilead isn't a  possible horrifying future but the  reality of what america has always been  elizabeth moss has garnered a ton of  attention for her stunning lead  performance as offred a role which  requires a lot of close-ups of her face  as she deals with hardship upon hardship  with each of the close-ups offred's  emotion whether it's sorrow rage or  rarely joy is crystal clear and what  becomes clearer to viewers with these  close-ups is alfred's lack of makeup  naturally a handmaid wouldn't be allowed  to wear it as vanity isn't an option for  them in the dystopian society a  character not wearing very much makeup  isn't new for film or television but  it's rare for an actor to not wear any  makeup at all minimal makeup is  typically applied on the actor for  filming purposes even if to give the  impression of no makeup but the  handmaid's tale uses a much more natural  approach and moss foregoes makeup for  most scenes if makeup is applied it's  typically to make the character of  offred known in her old life as june  look more tired moss told yahoo style in  2017 if you're not sleeping a lot  because you're working your butt off and  you get dark circles and bags under your  eyes the more you have the better you're  supposed to look stressed in fact one  night i got sleep i came in and my  makeup artist was like oh dear and was  disappointed that i didn't have the dark  circles under my eyes that we needed  the handmaid's tale is a great example  of how powerful voiceover can be when  used well june's voiceover gives the  audience an essential look into who she  is at her core rather than the identity  of offred that she's forced into as a  handmaid discussing his surprise over  how perfectly the voiceover worked for  the scenes bruce miller told vulture in  2017 we shot these bits of lizzy just  sitting in a room quietly going through  emotional phases and when i watched it  paired up with the voiceover it worked  perfectly and i said that's lucky except  it wasn't luck it turns out that moss  had memorized all of her voice over  dialogue so she could run it through her  head while filming the scenes to make  sure the narration would match the  setting take some  [Music]  i don't need  oranges i need to scream  but moss is a bit more modest about her  acting chops she told vulture it's a  part of the script why wouldn't i  memorize it how else am i supposed to  know what i'm thinking i'm sitting there  for 30 seconds i have to be thinking  about something i just thought that was  part of my job  upon its debut many people viewed the  handmaid's tale series as a response to  the 2016 election in which donald trump  was elected president trump has been  accused of sexual assault one of the  many horrors the handmaids must suffer  through the show touches on abortion and  other women's health issues which some  viewed as a direct criticism it's a  connection that can't be completely  ignored but it's also one that's mostly  not true the hollywood reporter pointed  out in 2017 that much of the first  season was completed before trump was  elected making it one of the most  unintentionally relevant shows in recent  years however when the cast and crew  returned for a second season the general  atmosphere was much different on set the  2016 election results couldn't be  ignored and headlines about the show  made the connection samira wiley who  plays june's close friend moira spoke  with vanity fair in 2017 about how the  election impacted everyone on set wiley  stated coming back to the show it was oh  my gosh we have an even bigger  responsibility now to have this show be  excellent have it have all the integrity  it should have  the handmaid's tale was an instant  success and with that came instant  recognition at the 2017 emmys the hulu  show swept the night with 13 nominations  the series won eight awards including  three acting categories directing  writing and production design the series  also won the coveted outstanding drama  series  with these wins the series became the  first show from a streaming service to  ever win the emmy for best drama series  with streaming services becoming more  prominent and continuing to produce  award-worthy content the handmaid's tale  could go down in the history books  backstage after the show's emmy wins  show creator bruce miller told deadline  the way that hulu handled our show they  were bold and behind us and really  committed to making something  interesting if the streaming services  continue to do that i don't see a limit  to how many emmys they can win  the first season of the handmaid's tale  covers all of the events of margaret  atwood's novel so the second season was  an unexpected continuation of the story  for fans and with season 5 on the way  bruce miller says he doesn't currently  have an end date in sight miller told  the hollywood reporter i certainly don't  have a number of seasons in mind it's  just that i'm finding more interesting  paths along the way and more interesting  things to do as we move towards more  fascinating parts of the story miller  continued by declaring that there was  another person vital in deciding how  many seasons the show would get he said  when i said that i'll do the show as  long as lizzie moss wants to do it that  is born of the pandemic and born of the  work she's done this year she deserves  to have me there writing for her as long  as she wants she earned that this year i  will write beautiful words for her to  say for as long as she wants to say them  it's clear miller is proud of the work  he's done with the handmaid's tale and  seems content on exploring the story for  as long as possible he told the dream  cage i've loved this story for 35 years  i thought it was amazing and i am lucky  and privileged to be part of it now  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

